<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">David of Burgundy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1427">1427</a>–<a href="/years/1496">1496</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> was an illegitimate son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a>, Duke of Burgundy, who had him made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Utrecht in 1456 against the chapter's own candidate. Previously bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/therouanne">Thérouanne</a>, he extended Burgundian influence over the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. His rule faced repeated revolts, above all in the Utrecht civil war of 1481–1483.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-of-burgundy.jpg" alt="David of Burgundy" width="320" height="280" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maestro_di_delft,_trittico_della_madonna_col_bambino_e_santi,_1500_ca._02_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
